@page "/"
@inject SiteInfo Site

<PageHead Title="Home" Description="@Site.Description" />

<div class="hero">
    <h1>Kiji</h1>
    <p class="lede">Write Razor. Publish a purely static site.</p>
    <p>Kiji renders your Razor components with Blazor's
        <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/aspnet/core/blazor/components/render-components-outside-of-aspnetcore"><code>HtmlRenderer</code></a>
        and writes each page, Markdown included, as plain HTML for any static host.</p>
</div>

<section class="pitch">
    <article>
        <h2><span class="heading-emoji" aria-hidden="true">🧰</span><span>Your .NET workflow</span></h2>
        <p>Build with Razor, C#, and standard .NET host builders. Preview via <code>dotnet watch</code>, publish via <code>dotnet publish</code>.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
        <h2><span class="heading-emoji" aria-hidden="true">📦</span><span>Publishing essentials included</span></h2>
        <p>Typed Markdown, responsive WebP images, RSS, and sitemaps in one package.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
        <h2><span class="heading-emoji" aria-hidden="true">⚡</span><span>Work on what changed</span></h2>
        <p>The browser reloads while you write, and publishing reuses unchanged pages.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
        <h2><span class="heading-emoji" aria-hidden="true">🔄</span><span>Make it yours</span></h2>
        <p>Customize Markdown parsing, add your own services and generated files, or replace the image processor.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
        <h2><span class="heading-emoji" aria-hidden="true">🔕</span><span>Static by design</span></h2>
        <p>The output needs no .NET server or Blazor runtime. Use JavaScript for browser
            interactivity; Razor event handlers such as <code>@@onclick</code> do not run in the browser.</p>
    </article>
</section>
